course: dictionary
lesson: 11079
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the words around them tell you which meaning you need. «Wellington is the capital of New Zealand.» The capital W shows that Wellington is a place name, and the word capital tells you it is a city.
explain: Now look at «She pulled on her wellingtons before splashing in the puddle.» Here wellingtons with a small w means tall rubber boots that keep your feet dry. The capital W and the small w tell you which meaning to pick, and the rest of the sentence makes it clear.
explain: The boots are named after the Duke of Wellington, a famous British general. Words can come from people's names, so a name can become an everyday word. Other places use other words too: Americans call these boots rain boots or rubber boots.
explain: Check your choice by putting the meaning into the sentence. If «A girl in Wellington hears the earthquake drill alarm» is read as «a girl in a tall rubber boot», it does not make sense, so the city meaning is the right one.
fact: A word can have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is meant.
fact: «Wellington» with a capital W is the capital city of New Zealand, at the southern tip of the North Island.
fact: «Wellingtons» (or informal «wellies») are tall rubber or plastic boots that keep your feet dry in rain and mud.
fact: The boots were named after the Duke of Wellington, a famous British general.
fact: Americans call these boots rain boots or rubber boots.
exercise: «Wellington is the capital of New Zealand.» What is Wellington here? | *The capital city of New Zealand | A kind of rain | A tall rubber boot | A famous British general
exercise: Ella says, «I put on my wellies before going out in the garden.» What are wellies? | A city in New Zealand | A famous general | *Tall rubber boots that keep your feet dry | A kind of wind
exercise: «Wellington is the capital of New Zealand.» Why does Wellington have a capital W? | It is a long word | It is a type of boot | *It is the name of a place | It is the first word of a sentence, so it is always capital
test: Mum says, «Pack your wellingtons, it is muddy on the farm.» What does wellingtons mean? | *Tall rubber boots | A city in New Zealand | A famous general | A kind of rain
test: «A girl in Wellington hears the earthquake drill alarm.» What does Wellington mean here? | A famous general | *The capital city of New Zealand | A tall rubber boot | A type of wind
exam: Which sentence uses wellingtons to mean boots? | *«She pulled her wellingtons on before splashing in the puddle.» | «Wellington is the capital of New Zealand.» | «A boy in Wellington hears the earthquake drill signal.»
